\@writefile{toc}{\contentsline {subsection}{\numberline {9.1}Language Constructs}{27}{subsection.9.1}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{language-constructs}{{9.1}{27}{Language Constructs}{subsection.9.1}{}}
\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {6}{\ignorespaces The GNN language constructs a conforming parser must recognize, with the meaning each carries. The first rows are the required sections that pin a model's identity, variables, and factor graph; the optional sections that follow carry parameterization, ontology bindings, timing, equations, and provenance. The operator rows are the connection vocabulary the \texttt {Connections} section is written in --- directed \texttt {A\textgreater {}B}, undirected \texttt {A-B}, and their labeled v1.1 forms --- and \texttt {default=…} supplies initialization hints. Definitions follow the GNN syntax specification and the exemplar specifications under \texttt {input/gnn\_files}, and the strict schema validator enforces section order and declaration grammar before any downstream step runs. The construct vocabulary is deliberately kind-agnostic: the same \texttt {StateSpaceBlock}, \texttt {Connections}, and operator syntax declare a discrete categorical model, a continuous linear-Gaussian model, or a composed multi-agent or hierarchical one --- what differs between kinds is which matrix names the block declares, as tbl.~\ref {tbl:actinf_symbols} details.}}{27}{table.6}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{tbl:gnn_constructs}{{6}{27}{The GNN language constructs a conforming parser must recognize, with the meaning each carries. The first rows are the required sections that pin a model's identity, variables, and factor graph; the optional sections that follow carry parameterization, ontology bindings, timing, equations, and provenance. The operator rows are the connection vocabulary the \protect \texttt {Connections} section is written in --- directed \protect \texttt {A\textgreater {}B}, undirected \protect \texttt {A-B}, and their labeled v1.1 forms --- and \protect \texttt {default=…} supplies initialization hints. Definitions follow the GNN syntax specification and the exemplar specifications under \protect \texttt {input/gnn\_files}, and the strict schema validator enforces section order and declaration grammar before any downstream step runs. The construct vocabulary is deliberately kind-agnostic: the same \protect \texttt {StateSpaceBlock}, \protect \texttt {Connections}, and operator syntax declare a discrete categorical model, a continuous linear-Gaussian model, or a composed multi-agent or hierarchical one --- what differs between kinds is which matrix names the block declares, as tbl.~\ref {tbl:actinf_symbols} details}{table.6}{}}
\citation{dacosta2020,smith2022,parr2022}
\citation{friston2010}
